  "dom_id": "step-q349983",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
